; TEST: expect-exit=100
;
; The two halves of spec 5.4's division decision (2026-09-25), checked as
; PROPERTIES rather than as tabulated constants:
;
;   the identity   a == (a/b)*b + (a rem b)
;   the sign rule  the remainder's sign follows the DIVIDEND
;
; The identity is computed in IR -- `div`, `rem`, `mul`, `add`, then
; `cmp.eq` against `a` -- once per pair of signs, so a lowering that got
; the quotient's rounding and the remainder's sign inconsistently right
; (floor one, truncate the other) fails the identity even where each
; separate value looked plausible.
;
;   check  a     b     div  rem  identity
;    1, 2  -17    5     -3   -2   -3*5 + -2 = -17
;    3, 4   17   -5     -3    2   -3*-5 + 2 = 17
;    5, 6  -17   -5      3   -2    3*-5 + -2 = -17
;    7, 8   17    5      3    2    3*5 + 2 = 17
;    9     -18    6      -3   0   an exact division: no sign to follow
;   10      -1    2 (i8)  0  -1   the same rule at a narrow width
;
; A FLOORING division would give -4 and 3 for check 1's pair and satisfy
; the identity just as well -- floor division has its own consistent
; remainder. What rules it out is the even-numbered checks, which pin the
; remainder itself: -2, not 3. Both halves are needed and both are here.
;
; Check 10 is at i8 so the result crosses the canonical form (ssa-ir.md
; 2.2): -1 rem 2 is -1, which must sit in the slot sign-extended, and the
; 64-bit `cmp.eq` against `iconst i8 -1` sees all 64 bits.
;
; Mutation (run): the remainder taken from the quotient's sign (negating
; rdx when the divisor is negative) -> exit 4. `rem` lowered as
; `a - (a/b)*b` with a FLOOR division -> exit 2.
;
; NO `c-emit-exit=`: the C backend lowers both opcodes (c-backend.md D4 rows
; 4 and 5), so the identity above is computed FIVE times per pair of signs --
; once by the reference and once by each of the four C builds -- and every one
; must reach 100. The C helpers divide MAGNITUDES and put the sign back
; (`exsi_mul_i`'s idiom), which is a different computation from the
; reference's IDIV; that they agree on all ten checks is the claim.

functio @initium (ptr) -> u8 {
b0:
%0 = param ptr 0
%1 = iconst i64 -17
%2 = iconst i64 5
%3 = div i64 %1 %2
%4 = rem i64 %1 %2
%5 = mul i64 %3 %2
%6 = add i64 %5 %4
%7 = cmp.eq i64 %6 %1
br %7 b2 b1
b1:
%8 = iconst u8 1
ret %8
b2:
%9 = iconst i64 -2
%10 = cmp.eq i64 %4 %9
br %10 b4 b3
b3:
%11 = iconst u8 2
ret %11
b4:
%12 = iconst i64 17
%13 = iconst i64 -5
%14 = div i64 %12 %13
%15 = rem i64 %12 %13
%16 = mul i64 %14 %13
%17 = add i64 %16 %15
%18 = cmp.eq i64 %17 %12
br %18 b6 b5
b5:
%19 = iconst u8 3
ret %19
b6:
%20 = iconst i64 2
%21 = cmp.eq i64 %15 %20
br %21 b8 b7
b7:
%22 = iconst u8 4
ret %22
b8:
%23 = iconst i64 -17
%24 = iconst i64 -5
%25 = div i64 %23 %24
%26 = rem i64 %23 %24
%27 = mul i64 %25 %24
%28 = add i64 %27 %26
%29 = cmp.eq i64 %28 %23
br %29 b10 b9
b9:
%30 = iconst u8 5
ret %30
b10:
%31 = iconst i64 -2
%32 = cmp.eq i64 %26 %31
br %32 b12 b11
b11:
%33 = iconst u8 6
ret %33
b12:
%34 = iconst i64 17
%35 = iconst i64 5
%36 = div i64 %34 %35
%37 = rem i64 %34 %35
%38 = mul i64 %36 %35
%39 = add i64 %38 %37
%40 = cmp.eq i64 %39 %34
br %40 b14 b13
b13:
%41 = iconst u8 7
ret %41
b14:
%42 = iconst i64 2
%43 = cmp.eq i64 %37 %42
br %43 b16 b15
b15:
%44 = iconst u8 8
ret %44
b16:
%45 = iconst i64 -18
%46 = iconst i64 6
%47 = rem i64 %45 %46
%48 = iconst i64 0
%49 = cmp.eq i64 %47 %48
br %49 b18 b17
b17:
%50 = iconst u8 9
ret %50
b18:
%51 = iconst i8 -1
%52 = iconst i8 2
%53 = rem i8 %51 %52
%54 = iconst i8 -1
%55 = cmp.eq i8 %53 %54
br %55 b20 b19
b19:
%56 = iconst u8 10
ret %56
b20:
%57 = iconst u8 100
ret %57
}
